# dark mode is fine for UI; it's wrong for sustained reading — here's why


# the evolutionary argument
#
# humans have read dark ink on light backgrounds for essentially all of recorded
# history — paper, vellum, stone, clay. your visual system evolved and trained
# on that contrast polarity. light-on-dark is a ~40-year-old artifact of early
# CRT phosphor screens, not a natural reading state.


# halation / blur
#
# white text on a dark background causes halation — bright edges bleed into the
# surrounding dark field due to how photoreceptors respond to contrast polarity.
# fine for glancing at a UI element; fatiguing for dense prose read over an hour.
# dark text on light doesn't produce the same effect because the light background
# scatters more uniformly.


# why PDFs and ebooks feel wrong in dark mode
#
# those formats were designed with print in mind. the spatial relationships,
# margins, line lengths, and contrast ratios all assume dark-on-light. inverting
# them isn't dark mode — it's fighting the artifact's design intent. it genuinely
# is wrong in a literal sense, not just a preference.


# the nuance
#
# dark mode makes sense for glanceable UI: dashboards, terminals, code editors
# late at night. for sustained reading — books, long docs, articles — light
# background wins on reading-fatigue measures consistently.
#
# if pure white feels harsh (especially on an OLED or in a dim room), a slightly
# warm or sepia tone is the right move, not inverting to dark.


# the rule of thumb
#
# dark mode: code, terminals, UI you scan
# light mode (or sepia): text you actually want to absorb


# does this apply to long webpages and blogs?
#
# yes — same logic. the halation and contrast-polarity arguments apply equally
# to a long blog post or documentation page as to a PDF. the wrinkle is you
# can't always control it.
#
# pdfs and ebooks you set once. webpages are styled by whoever built them.
# some sites force dark backgrounds with no override, or look broken when you
# apply a browser-level "force light mode" flag, or use images designed for
# dark that invert badly.


# practical toolkit for web reading
#
# safari reader view  — strips the page to clean light-background prose, your
#                       font, your size. best single move for long articles.
# firefox reader view — same; lets you pick sepia / light / dark per-session.
# browser force-light — works on most sites, occasionally breaks layouts.
# macos light appearance — well-behaved sites follow the system setting
#                          automatically; sets a sane default without per-site
#                          work.


# the practical split
#
# dark mode: OS shell, terminal, code editor
# light / reader view: anything you're reading at length
#
# you don't have to pick one globally — let the use case drive it.
